<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tondaimandalam</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tondai Nadu</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tondaimandalam is a historical region of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a>, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and including the area of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vellore">Vellore</a>. It was the heartland of the Pallava dynasty from the fourth to the ninth century and was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> around 900, under whom it was known as Jayankonda Cholamandalam.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tondaimandalam.jpg" alt="Tondaimandalam" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nesa raja sekaran · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tondai_Nadu_AP%2BTN_(NRS).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The region's borders were never fixed and probably changed over time. In general it took in the basins of three river systems: the Arani in the north, the Kosasthalaiyar in the middle and the Palar-Cheyyar-Veghavati in the south. Its northern edge lay roughly at Pulicat Lake and its southern somewhere north of the Pennai river. It included the Perumbanappadi chieftaincy in the northwest and part of Pangalanadu, and bordered the Vanakoppadi chieftaincy to the southwest and Naduvilnadu to the south.</p><p>Later records divide it into 24 kottams, districts subdivided into nadus, groups of agricultural villages, though only 22 kottams are attested in contemporary sources and the figure of 24 may be conventional. Eyilkottam contained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Burial urns, cairn circles and jar burials from about the start of the common era have been found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a>. In the early historical period the region was largely pastoral, broken by hills and forest into small pockets of farmland. The Sangam poem Perumpanarruppatai, written about 190-200 CE, describes large tracts of unsettled land with few settlements, and Pallava inscriptions often mention <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> given to temples or taken in raids. In the 3rd century CE it was ruled by Ilandiraiyan, the first king with the title Tondaiman, whom P. T. Srinivasa Iyengar identifies with a Pallava prince.</p><p>Under the Pallavas intensive farming and towns spread. From the 7th to the 9th centuries brahmadeya villages, tax-free grants to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, came with irrigation works kept up by Brahmin assemblies, and in the 9th and 10th centuries grants to temples drove further expansion. The number of nadus grew steadily from the 7th to the 12th century. Trade served the royal court and was conducted mostly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kanchipuram">Kanchipuram</a> and its port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamallapuram">Mamallapuram</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aditya-i">Aditya I</a> took the region from the Pallava ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aparajitavarman">Aparajitavarman</a>. Under Uttama Chola most of it was recovered from the Rashtrakutas, and Raja Raja Chola I gave it its Chola name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jatavarman-sundara-pandya-i">Jatavarman Sundara Pandyan I</a> conquered it as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nellore">Nellore</a> in 1258, and after the fall of the Pandyan Empire it came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madurai-sultanate">Madurai Sultanate</a> in 1323. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumara-kampana">Kumara Kampana</a> defeated the sultanate in 1361, and the region became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire</a>, later passing to the Nawabdom of the Carnatic and, under the British, to the Madras Presidency.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tondaimandalam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Aditya I defeated the Pallava and Pandyan dynasties early in the 10th century, capturing <b>Tondaimandalam</b> and Kongu Nadu, and Aditya's son Parantaka I defeated the Rashtrakuta dynasty in the battle of Vallala and the Pandyas again.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>They emerged as the Satavahana dynasty and its successor in the east, the Ikshvaku dynasty, declined, and their heartland was <b>Tondaimandalam</b>, the country around Kanchipuram in the north of present-day Tamil Nadu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Pratihara Mahipala I. The last great commander, Krishna III, allied with the Western Ganga Butuga II, defeated the Chola Empire at the Battle of Takkolam in 949, where the Chola prince Rajaditya was killed on his elephant, and occupied the <b>Tondaimandalam</b> region around Kanchi for several years.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rashtrakuta-dynasty">Rashtrakuta dynasty · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…outlived it: the Cholas ruled in central Tamil Nadu until the 13th century, the Cheras until the 12th, and the Pandyas parts of South India until the late 17th century, while <b>Tondai Nadu</b>, once under the Cholas, emerged as the independent Pallava realm by the 6th century CE.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sangam-age-tamilakam">Sangam-age Tamilakam · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
